## LAYOUT
- Use the SAME page container on EVERY page so the reading column sits in the same position throughout the book. Put the section content in one fixed-width column — `mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl` — with the same horizontal padding on every page (e.g. `px-8 max-sm:px-5`). NEVER vary the max-width (do not use max-w-2xl/3xl/4xl on some pages and max-w-5xl on others) or the padding between pages. Top-align content; vertically center (`items-center`) ONLY on cover / title sections.
- Recurring page chrome is part of the book's identity, not optional decoration. Inspect the corresponding vertical region of the original page for persistent side rails/bands, tinted page edges, border strips, corner ornaments, and repeated header/footer rules. Reproduce every chrome element that intersects this section, using the original side, approximate width, color, opacity/gradient, and spacing. If adjacent regions of the original continue the same rail, make it span this section's FULL height with no section margin, rounded end, or vertical gap so separately generated sections join into one continuous page edge. Do not invent a rail or color on a page whose reference is plain/uncolored.
- Implement purely decorative chrome as elements with `aria-hidden="true"` and `pointer-events-none`; it must not receive focus, duplicate text, enter the accessibility tree, or convey information that is unavailable in text. Keep the section `relative`, place edge chrome behind content, and reserve enough inline padding that it never covers text, images, form controls, focus rings, or answer spaces. At mobile and 200% zoom, a rail may become narrower or a subtle border, but it must retain the book's recognizable color without stealing usable reading width. Do not use `overflow-hidden` on the content section merely to contain decoration, because that can clip focus indicators and enlarged content.
- Reproduce the book's repeated semantic visual vocabulary as well: chapter banners, activity/exercise bars, callout fills, border colors, and heading accents must use the same role-to-color mapping seen in the original. Color is supplementary only—keep explicit text headings/labels, shapes/borders, and sufficient foreground contrast, and never make meaning depend on color alone.
- Full-bleed header bands: a top header/banner or colored strip MAY span the full page width so its background color bleeds edge-to-edge, but its TEXT and any badge MUST sit in the SAME `max-w-5xl mx-auto` column (same horizontal padding) as the body, so the title lines up with the body text below it. Put the banner's inner content in `<div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl px-8 max-sm:px-5">…</div>`. Let the banner size to its content — do NOT give it a fixed height or `overflow-hidden` that clips the title or badge. Place the chapter badge/circle INSIDE the banner's own flex row (normal flow, e.g. title on the left and badge on the right); never `absolute`-position the badge so it overlaps the body content below.
- Keep the running body text as ONE continuous column in reading order. Do NOT split it into a side-by-side row (body text beside a sidebar) followed by a separate full-width block underneath — when the sidebar is taller than the top text, that leaves a large empty gap on the page. This is the single most common layout mistake; avoid it.
- When the section pairs running text with a narrow callout/sidebar panel (e.g. Vocabulary, Big Question, a tip box), FLOAT the panel so the text wraps beside it and then flows full-width below it with no gap. Emit, in this order: any full-width header/banner, then the floated panel, then ALL body paragraphs as normal siblings. Example:
  `<aside class="float-right w-[34%] ml-8 mb-6 max-lg:float-none max-lg:w-full max-lg:ml-0"> …panel… </aside>` followed by the `<p class="adt-body">` paragraphs in reading order.
- A wide element that must sit BELOW everything (a full-width image or diagram) should clear the float with `clear-both`.
- Use a real multi-column split (flex/grid) ONLY when both columns hold comparable, self-contained content (e.g. a paired image + its explanation) — never for a short sidebar beside long running text.
- Size content images (photos, diagrams, illustrations) to the layout, matching how prominently they appear in the original page image. A standalone image should fill the content column (`w-full h-auto`); images paired in a row each fill their share of the row. Do NOT render content images as small thumbnails — prefer filling the available width even if it means modestly upscaling a low-resolution source. Keep an image small ONLY if it is a genuinely small inline icon or decoration in the original.
- Text baked into an image: if an image already contains legible text (a labeled diagram, a poster, a text graphic) and that same text is also provided as an associated text leaf, do NOT show the words twice. Render the image on its own, and emit the associated text leaf as VISUALLY HIDDEN with `sr-only` (it stays available to read-aloud and screen readers but is not drawn on screen). Never overlay or caption a transcription on top of text that is already legible inside the image. Only overlay/caption text on an image when the words are NOT already present in the image.

## LABELED DIAGRAMS (VISUAL FIDELITY + INCLUSIVE ACCESS)
A labeled diagram is an `image_group` containing an illustration/image plus two or more `label` leaves and usually a caption. Treat it as one semantic visual, not as an ordinary image followed by disposable text.

- Inspect the PROVIDED EXTRACTED IMAGE itself, separately from the full-page reference. PDF extraction often separates vector/OCR labels and leader lines from the raster illustration. Labels visible on the full page are NOT necessarily baked into the provided image crop.
- If a provided label is absent from the extracted image pixels, it MUST remain visibly rendered around or beside the diagram. Never put those labels in `sr-only`. Reconstruct the source's callout arrangement with the label leaves.
- A leader line is a factual connection, not decoration. Every visible leader must be continuous from the visible edge of its label to the exact referenced feature. Trace the corresponding callout in the ORIGINAL PAGE IMAGE and copy that label-to-feature relationship; do not infer a plausible organ location from general knowledge. BOTH ends must visibly touch: the label-side endpoint must meet (or underlap by 2–4 CSS px beneath) the label container with zero whitespace gap, and the feature-end must coincide with its named endpoint dot. The marker's `aria-label` must exactly equal its linked label text. Give each linked label anchor an opaque background matching its surrounding panel plus small horizontal padding (for example `relative z-30 bg-white px-1`), so the underlap is masked at the label edge and can never strike through the label glyphs. Every leader SVG MUST preserve its aspect ratio; `preserveAspectRatio="none"` is forbidden because it stretches coordinates away from the image features. A short horizontal rule that stops in empty space, a disconnected line segment, or a line that merely points toward the image is FORBIDDEN.
- When the original page visibly uses leader lines and their feature endpoints are discernible, preserving those connections is REQUIRED. Prefer ONE unified, aspect-ratio-preserving SVG coordinate system containing the image, visible labels, leaders, and markers together. Put the provided `<img data-id="...">` inside an SVG `<foreignObject>` and position it in the center of the viewBox; place every linked label as an SVG `<text data-id="...">` and every leader/marker in that SAME SVG. Use an SVG `<a id="..." href="#marker-id">` around each label text. This makes label positions and anatomical endpoints scale together without drift. Give label text a background-colored stroke halo (`paint-order="stroke"`, matching `stroke`, and a positive `stroke-width`) so no leader crosses its glyphs. Never position one global SVG independently from HTML labels. The alternative per-label zero-gap callout pattern is allowed only when its inline SVG genuinely spans from the adjacent label edge all the way to the named feature.
- If exact feature coordinates genuinely cannot be established from the original, do NOT draw leader lines. Put numbered high-contrast markers on confidently identifiable regions and use the same numbers in a visible semantic parts key. If even marker placement is uncertain, use a plain semantic parts list beside/below the image. A fallback list must not contain trailing borders, flex-grow rules, or decorative strokes that resemble unfinished callouts. It is better to show an honest accessible key than a misleading spatial connection.
- Use a semantic `<figure data-labeled-diagram>` containing the image, the visible label structure, and a `<figcaption>`. Give the image a concise non-empty `alt` derived from the provided caption/heading (do not invent scientific claims).
- Put all label leaves in one semantic `<ul aria-label="Diagram parts">`; each label remains inside its exact provided `data-id`. CSS grid/flex may position list items around the image on desktop, but DOM order must remain logical and the labels must reflow into a single readable list below the image at `max-sm:`.
- When the corresponding part location can be identified confidently from the page reference, make the visible label an anchor to a focusable endpoint marker over the image. The anchor and marker use a unique, section-scoped fragment ID; the marker has an accessible name matching the label and a visible focus/target ring. The marker must coincide with the visible leader endpoint or numbered region marker. This gives keyboard and screen-reader users an explicit label-to-region relationship without custom scripts. If placement is uncertain, keep a plain semantic list item rather than inventing a wrong hotspot.
- Required unified structure example (use measured coordinates from the original page): `<svg viewBox="0 0 1000 700" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" class="w-full h-auto" role="group" aria-label="Diagram parts"><foreignObject x="250" y="0" width="500" height="700"><img xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" data-id="provided-image-id" src="..." alt="..." class="w-full h-full object-contain"></foreignObject><a id="sec-part-label" href="#sec-part"><text data-id="provided-label-id" x="40" y="120" paint-order="stroke" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="8" fill="#222">Part</text></a><polyline data-label-id="provided-label-id" data-label-contact="start" aria-labelledby="sec-part-label sec-part" points="labelEdgeX,120 bendX,120 partX,partY" fill="none" stroke="#334155"/><circle id="sec-part" tabindex="0" aria-label="Part" cx="partX" cy="partY" r="6"/></svg>`. The polyline label-side point must visibly meet the text's outside edge; its opposite endpoint must exactly equal the feature circle coordinates. Repeat inside the same SVG for every label. Never use `preserveAspectRatio="none"`, separate `h-px`, borders, or flex-rule spans.
- Callout leaders and markers are supplementary: label/part identity must never depend on color, pointer position, hover, or spatial layout alone. Use text, borders/shapes, and strong contrast. Do not require a mouse.
- At narrow widths and 200% zoom, prioritize the full diagram followed by the structured parts list; do not preserve desktop overlays if they cause clipping, overlap, tiny targets, or horizontal scrolling.
- Only use `sr-only` for an associated label after verifying that the exact label is visibly legible inside the PROVIDED EXTRACTED IMAGE. Even then, keep the figure's caption and useful non-empty image alternative.
## RESPONSIVE DESIGN (DESKTOP-FIRST)
Your HTML will be tested at these viewport widths. Use ONLY these Tailwind responsive prefixes:
{% for vp in viewports %}- **{{ vp.label }}** ({{ vp.width }}px){% if vp.tailwind_prefix != "" %}: `{{ vp.tailwind_prefix }}` prefix{% else %}: default (no prefix — base/desktop styles){% endif %}
{% endfor %}
Do NOT use `sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:`, `2xl:`, or any other prefixes not listed above.
The base (no-prefix) classes are the DESKTOP design. Use `max-lg:` to override styles at tablet and below, and `max-sm:` to override at mobile only. On smaller viewports, favor single-column stacked layouts — switch multi-column desktop layouts to a stacked column with `max-lg:flex-col`.
Example: `class="flex flex-row max-lg:flex-col"` or `class="gap-8 max-lg:gap-4 max-sm:gap-2"`
